{"module":"systemic","voice":"male","duration":247.2,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Systemic racism: how institutions carry it","start":0.0,"end":3.36,"words":[[0.04,0.64],[0.64,1.31],[1.57,1.79],[1.79,2.69],[2.69,2.98],[2.98,3.13]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Many people believe racism ended when the laws that named race were repealed, and that what's left is a few bad individuals.","start":4.16,"end":12.63,"words":[[4.22,4.54],[4.54,4.95],[4.95,5.36],[5.36,6.01],[6.01,6.42],[6.42,6.66],[6.66,6.83],[6.83,7.07],[7.07,7.32],[7.32,7.72],[7.72,8.05],[8.05,8.21],[8.21,8.78],[9.07,9.31],[9.31,9.56],[9.56,9.89],[9.89,10.21],[10.21,10.38],[10.38,10.54],[10.54,10.78],[10.78,11.03],[11.03,12.09]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Systemic racism means something different: rules and institutions that keep producing unequal results even when no one in them intends to discriminate.","start":12.98,"end":22.58,"words":[[13.02,13.6],[13.6,14.17],[14.17,14.46],[14.46,14.89],[14.89,15.47],[15.72,16.01],[16.01,16.23],[16.23,17.09],[17.09,17.31],[17.31,17.53],[17.53,18.18],[18.18,18.68],[18.68,19.18],[19.18,19.47],[19.47,19.69],[19.69,19.91],[19.91,20.12],[20.12,20.27],[20.27,20.48],[20.48,20.99],[20.99,21.13],[21.13,22.07]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Individual and institutional","start":22.93,"end":25.22,"words":[[22.98,23.82],[23.82,24.05],[24.05,25.04]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In 1967 Stokely Carmichael (later Kwame Ture) and political scientist Charles V.","start":25.92,"end":32.39,"words":[[25.98,26.12],[26.12,27.42],[27.42,27.93],[27.93,28.51],[28.51,28.87],[28.87,29.23],[29.23,29.52],[29.77,29.99],[29.99,30.64],[30.64,31.29],[31.29,31.65],[31.65,31.86]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Hamilton introduced the term “institutional racism” in Black Power.","start":32.74,"end":37.07,"words":[[32.78,33.32],[33.32,34.0],[34.0,34.14],[34.14,34.34],[34.34,35.23],[35.23,35.78],[35.78,35.91],[35.91,36.18],[36.18,36.53]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Their example: when white terrorists bomb a Black church, everyone sees it.","start":37.42,"end":42.61,"words":[[37.46,37.67],[37.67,38.33],[38.58,38.8],[38.8,39.09],[39.09,39.75],[39.75,39.97],[39.97,40.11],[40.11,40.4],[40.4,40.84],[41.1,41.6],[41.6,41.82],[41.82,42.04]]},{"el":"s0","text":"When hundreds of Black babies in Birmingham die each year from lack of food, shelter and medical care, that is racism too, but it works through the ordinary operation of institutions, and few people see it.","start":42.96,"end":56.64,"words":[[43.01,43.24],[43.24,43.88],[43.88,44.04],[44.04,44.36],[44.36,44.84],[44.84,45.0],[45.0,45.55],[45.55,45.79],[45.79,46.03],[46.03,46.27],[46.27,46.59],[46.59,46.83],[46.83,46.99],[46.99,47.3],[47.58,47.98],[47.98,48.22],[48.22,48.78],[48.78,49.09],[49.37,49.61],[49.61,49.77],[49.77,50.41],[50.41,50.65],[50.92,51.16],[51.16,51.32],[51.32,51.64],[51.64,51.88],[51.88,52.04],[52.04,52.6],[52.6,53.31],[53.31,53.47],[53.47,54.51],[54.78,55.02],[55.02,55.26],[55.26,55.66],[55.66,55.82],[55.82,56.06]]},{"el":"h1","text":"How neutral rules carry history forward","start":57.54,"end":60.39,"words":[[57.57,57.84],[57.84,58.46],[58.46,58.81],[58.81,59.16],[59.16,59.78],[59.78,60.22]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Schools funded by local property taxes inherit the value gaps that redlining created (see “Place” and “Schools”).","start":61.09,"end":67.76,"words":[[61.14,61.48],[61.48,61.9],[61.9,62.11],[62.11,62.52],[62.52,63.0],[63.0,63.42],[63.42,63.9],[63.9,64.04],[64.04,64.39],[64.39,64.66],[64.66,64.87],[64.87,65.49],[65.49,66.11],[66.11,66.25],[66.25,66.6],[66.6,66.8],[66.8,67.22]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Credit scores reward a history of borrowing from mainstream banks, which Black families were long denied (see “Banking”).","start":68.11,"end":76.14,"words":[[68.16,68.65],[68.65,68.97],[68.97,69.38],[69.38,69.54],[69.54,70.11],[70.11,70.27],[70.27,70.84],[70.84,71.16],[71.16,71.89],[71.89,72.37],[72.66,72.98],[72.98,73.31],[73.31,73.87],[73.87,74.03],[74.03,74.28],[74.28,74.76],[74.76,74.93],[74.93,75.49]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Legacy admissions favor children of alumni at colleges that excluded Black students for generations.","start":76.49,"end":83.44,"words":[[76.54,76.98],[76.98,77.58],[77.58,77.95],[77.95,78.55],[78.55,78.7],[78.7,79.14],[79.14,79.29],[79.29,79.89],[79.89,80.11],[80.11,80.78],[80.78,81.08],[81.08,81.75],[81.75,81.9],[81.9,82.87]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Felony disenfranchisement applies to everyone, but falls on communities policed most heavily (see “The vote”).","start":83.79,"end":90.89,"words":[[83.81,84.26],[84.26,85.6],[85.6,86.05],[86.05,86.2],[86.2,86.8],[87.06,87.28],[87.28,87.58],[87.58,87.73],[87.73,88.48],[88.48,88.93],[88.93,89.3],[89.3,89.75],[89.75,89.9],[89.9,90.05],[90.05,90.42]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Zoning for large lots and single-family homes keeps out people who couldn't build wealth.","start":91.24,"end":96.71,"words":[[91.29,91.75],[91.75,91.9],[91.9,92.2],[92.2,92.5],[92.5,92.72],[92.72,93.62],[93.62,94.0],[94.0,94.3],[94.3,94.52],[94.52,94.9],[94.9,95.05],[95.05,95.5],[95.5,95.8],[95.8,96.18]]},{"el":"s1","text":"None of these rules mentions race.","start":97.06,"end":99.78,"words":[[97.1,97.37],[97.37,97.54],[97.54,97.8],[97.8,98.15],[98.15,98.76],[98.76,99.19]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Each was shaped by, and keeps passing along, decisions that did.","start":100.13,"end":105.08,"words":[[100.18,100.43],[100.43,100.67],[100.67,101.08],[101.08,101.41],[101.69,101.94],[101.94,102.26],[102.26,102.67],[102.67,103.08],[103.37,104.02],[104.02,104.27],[104.27,104.59]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Intent versus impact in the law","start":105.98,"end":108.79,"words":[[106.03,106.71],[106.71,107.28],[107.28,107.96],[107.96,108.19],[108.19,108.41],[108.41,108.64]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In Griggs v. Duke Power (1971) the Supreme Court held that job requirements with a discriminatory effect violate the Civil Rights Act unless they're truly needed for the job (“disparate impact”).","start":109.49,"end":122.04,"words":[[109.54,109.68],[109.68,110.04],[110.04,110.25],[110.61,110.9],[110.9,111.18],[111.18,112.4],[112.65,112.79],[112.79,113.22],[113.22,113.44],[113.44,113.72],[113.72,113.94],[113.94,114.22],[114.22,115.08],[115.08,115.3],[115.3,115.44],[115.44,116.44],[116.44,116.8],[116.8,117.37],[117.37,117.52],[117.52,117.87],[117.87,118.23],[118.23,118.45],[118.45,118.8],[118.8,119.09],[119.09,119.45],[119.45,119.81],[119.81,119.95],[119.95,120.09],[120.09,120.38],[120.38,120.95],[120.95,121.45]]},{"el":"s2","text":"But in Washington v. Davis (1976) it held that the Constitution forbids only intentional discrimination.","start":122.39,"end":130.33,"words":[[122.44,122.64],[122.64,122.78],[122.78,123.33],[123.33,123.54],[123.88,124.29],[124.29,125.53],[125.77,125.9],[125.9,126.18],[126.18,126.38],[126.38,126.52],[126.52,127.35],[127.35,127.76],[127.76,128.1],[128.1,128.79],[128.79,129.82]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In McCleskey v. Kemp (1987) the Court accepted a study showing that killers of white victims in Georgia were far more likely to be sentenced to death, and upheld the sentence anyway, because McCleskey couldn't prove intent in his own case.","start":130.68,"end":147.7,"words":[[130.72,130.88],[130.88,131.54],[131.54,131.78],[132.19,132.52],[132.52,133.83],[134.12,134.28],[134.28,134.53],[134.53,135.18],[135.18,135.35],[135.35,135.76],[135.76,136.17],[136.17,136.41],[136.41,136.82],[136.82,136.99],[136.99,137.31],[137.31,137.89],[137.89,138.05],[138.05,138.54],[138.54,138.71],[138.71,138.87],[138.87,139.03],[139.03,139.52],[139.52,139.69],[139.69,139.85],[139.85,140.51],[140.51,140.67],[140.67,141.0],[141.29,141.53],[141.53,142.02],[142.02,142.19],[142.19,142.76],[142.76,143.33],[143.62,144.03],[144.03,144.69],[144.69,145.18],[145.18,145.5],[145.5,146.0],[146.0,146.16],[146.16,146.41],[146.41,146.65],[146.65,147.06]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Alexander v. Sandoval (2001) barred private lawsuits over disparate impact under Title VI.","start":148.05,"end":155.24,"words":[[148.1,148.75],[148.75,148.97],[149.33,149.91],[149.91,150.78],[151.04,151.25],[151.25,151.76],[151.76,152.2],[152.2,152.49],[152.49,153.07],[153.07,153.5],[153.5,153.79],[153.79,154.23],[154.23,154.66]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Court did uphold disparate-impact claims under the Fair Housing Act in 2015, and a 2025 executive order directed federal agencies to stop using disparate-impact liability.","start":155.59,"end":168.11,"words":[[155.64,155.78],[155.78,155.99],[155.99,156.21],[156.21,156.71],[156.71,157.72],[157.72,158.15],[158.15,158.44],[158.44,158.58],[158.58,158.8],[158.8,159.23],[159.23,159.44],[159.44,159.59],[159.59,160.52],[160.77,160.99],[160.99,161.13],[161.13,162.28],[162.28,163.0],[163.0,163.22],[163.22,163.86],[163.86,164.37],[164.37,165.01],[165.01,165.16],[165.16,165.44],[165.44,165.8],[165.8,166.81],[166.81,167.6]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Because intent is hard to prove, much systemic discrimination is legal.","start":168.46,"end":173.25,"words":[[168.51,168.86],[168.86,169.29],[169.29,169.43],[169.43,169.64],[169.64,169.78],[169.78,170.13],[170.38,170.66],[170.66,171.22],[171.22,172.21],[172.21,172.35],[172.35,172.77]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Do disparities prove discrimination?","start":174.15,"end":176.91,"words":[[174.16,174.3],[174.3,175.01],[175.01,175.29],[175.29,176.36]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Economists such as Thomas Sowell and Glenn Loury argue that group differences can come from many causes, such as age, region, family structure, culture and education, and that disparities alone don't prove present-day discrimination.","start":177.61,"end":193.87,"words":[[177.66,178.43],[178.43,178.74],[178.74,178.89],[178.89,179.28],[179.28,179.66],[179.66,179.89],[179.89,180.2],[180.2,180.51],[180.51,180.82],[180.82,181.05],[181.05,181.36],[181.36,182.05],[182.05,182.28],[182.28,182.51],[182.51,182.82],[182.82,183.13],[183.13,183.59],[183.86,184.17],[184.17,184.33],[184.33,184.71],[184.98,185.52],[185.79,186.25],[186.25,186.95],[187.22,187.68],[187.68,187.91],[187.91,188.76],[189.03,189.26],[189.26,189.49],[189.49,190.26],[190.26,190.65],[190.65,191.03],[191.03,191.34],[191.34,192.11],[192.11,193.27]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Others answer that many of those “other causes” are themselves results of past discrimination.","start":194.22,"end":199.83,"words":[[194.27,194.58],[194.58,194.88],[194.88,195.11],[195.11,195.41],[195.41,195.56],[195.56,195.87],[195.87,196.1],[196.1,196.48],[196.48,196.55],[196.55,197.16],[197.16,197.69],[197.69,197.84],[197.84,198.14],[198.14,199.28]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Both sides can agree on one test: experiments that hold everything else equal and change only race.","start":200.18,"end":207.87,"words":[[200.23,200.57],[200.57,200.98],[200.98,201.24],[201.24,201.57],[201.57,201.74],[201.74,201.99],[201.99,202.41],[202.7,203.7],[203.7,203.95],[203.95,204.37],[204.37,204.96],[204.96,205.21],[205.21,205.63],[205.63,205.88],[205.88,206.46],[206.46,206.88],[206.88,207.3]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Those audit studies, in hiring, housing, lending, medicine and more, keep finding discrimination (see “Is racism over?”).","start":208.22,"end":215.73,"words":[[208.27,208.52],[208.52,208.78],[208.78,209.22],[209.44,209.57],[209.57,210.08],[210.3,210.75],[210.97,211.41],[211.63,212.02],[212.02,212.21],[212.21,212.4],[212.62,212.81],[212.81,213.25],[213.25,214.14],[214.14,214.27],[214.27,214.4],[214.4,214.91],[214.91,215.22]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Where to look","start":216.63,"end":217.55,"words":[[216.69,216.96],[216.96,217.14],[217.14,217.42]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The other modules trace systemic racism through each institution: government and law (Reconstruction, the vote), police and courts (“Policing,” “Courts,” “Incarceration”), banking (“Banking,” “Wealth”), medicine (“Science,” “Health”), schools, housing (“Place”), the environment, the media and the military.","start":218.25,"end":237.51,"words":[[218.3,218.45],[218.45,218.68],[218.68,219.21],[219.21,219.59],[219.59,220.19],[220.19,220.8],[220.8,221.03],[221.03,221.25],[221.25,222.16],[222.43,223.11],[223.11,223.34],[223.34,223.49],[223.49,224.55],[224.82,224.97],[224.97,225.35],[225.61,225.99],[225.99,226.22],[226.22,226.52],[226.52,227.13],[227.13,227.51],[227.51,228.49],[228.76,229.21],[229.21,229.74],[229.74,230.12],[230.39,230.84],[230.84,231.37],[231.37,231.75],[232.02,232.47],[232.74,233.19],[233.19,233.65],[233.91,234.07],[234.07,235.05],[235.32,235.47],[235.47,235.92],[235.92,236.15],[236.15,236.3],[236.3,236.98]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The CAREN Act targets one link in the chain, the moment a private person uses the police as a weapon, but it sits inside all of them.","start":237.86,"end":246.3,"words":[[237.91,238.05],[238.05,238.42],[238.42,238.64],[238.64,239.08],[239.08,239.3],[239.3,239.6],[239.6,239.74],[239.74,239.89],[239.89,240.33],[240.59,240.73],[240.73,241.25],[241.25,241.39],[241.39,241.91],[241.91,242.27],[242.27,242.64],[242.64,242.79],[242.79,243.15],[243.15,243.3],[243.3,243.45],[243.45,243.89],[244.14,244.36],[244.36,244.51],[244.51,244.8],[244.8,245.25],[245.25,245.39],[245.39,245.54],[245.54,245.83]]}]}